-- title:
--   The polytabloid and the rank-one column antisymmetrizer on the tabloid permutation module
-- statement:
--   This file constructs the permutation module on tabloids, the column antisymmetrizer and the polytabloid of a Young diagram, and shows that the antisymmetrizer is the rank-one operator $f\mapsto\langle e_\mu,f\rangle e_\mu$ determined by the polytabloid.
--
--   **Setting.** Throughout, $\mu$ is a Young diagram, regarded as a finite set $[\mu]\subset\mathbb{N}\times\mathbb{N}$ of cells $(i,j)$ (row $i$, column $j$, both counted from $0$) that is closed under decreasing either coordinate. Write $\operatorname{row}(i,j)=i$ and $\operatorname{col}(i,j)=j$, let $\mu_i$ be the length of row $i$, and put $n=|[\mu]|$. The cells serve both as positions in the diagram and as the $n$ letters permuted by the symmetric group $\mathrm{Sym}([\mu])$. A *tabloid* of shape $\mu$ is a map $r:[\mu]\to\mathbb{N}$ of the form $r=\operatorname{row}\circ p$ for some $p\in\mathrm{Sym}([\mu])$: it places each letter $x$ in row $r(x)$, and row $i$ receives exactly $\mu_i$ letters. Let $\mathrm{Tab}(\mu)$ be the (finite) set of tabloids. The *canonical tabloid* is $r_0=\operatorname{row}$ (each letter sits in its own row), and a permutation $g$ acts on tabloids by $g\cdot r=r\circ g^{-1}$. The *column group* $C_\mu$ is the subgroup of permutations $c\in\mathrm{Sym}([\mu])$ with $\operatorname{col}(c(x))=\operatorname{col}(x)$ for every cell $x$, i.e. those that move each letter only within its own column. A function $f$ on tabloids is *alternating* if $f(c\cdot r)=\operatorname{sgn}(c)\,f(r)$ for all $c\in C_\mu$ and all tabloids $r$, where $\operatorname{sgn}$ is the sign of a permutation regarded as a complex number.
--
--   1. **Permutation module.** $M^\mu=\mathbb{C}^{\mathrm{Tab}(\mu)}$ is the space of complex functions on tabloids, with the inner product $\langle f,h\rangle=\sum_{r\in\mathrm{Tab}(\mu)}\overline{f(r)}\,h(r)$, on which $\mathrm{Sym}([\mu])$ acts by the representation
--   $$
--   (\rho_\mu(g)f)(r)=f(g^{-1}\cdot r).
--   $$
--   This representation is unitary: $\langle\rho_\mu(g)f,\rho_\mu(g)h\rangle=\langle f,h\rangle$ for all $g\in\mathrm{Sym}([\mu])$ and $f,h\in M^\mu$.
--
--   2. **Column antisymmetrizer.** $A_\mu=\sum_{c\in C_\mu}\operatorname{sgn}(c)\,\rho_\mu(c)$, the linear operator on $M^\mu$ given by $(A_\mu f)(r)=\sum_{c\in C_\mu}\operatorname{sgn}(c)\,f(c^{-1}\cdot r)$. For every $f\in M^\mu$ the function $A_\mu f$ is alternating.
--
--   3. **Polytabloid.** Let $\delta_r\in M^\mu$ be the indicator function of the single tabloid $r$. The polytabloid is $e_\mu=A_\mu\delta_{r_0}$. For $c\in C_\mu$ one has $c\cdot r_0=r_0$ if and only if $c=1$; moreover $e_\mu(r_0)=1$, so $e_\mu\neq0$, and $e_\mu$ is alternating.
--
--   4. **Rank-one formula.** For all $f,h\in M^\mu$ and every tabloid $r$: $A_\mu f=(A_\mu f)(r_0)\,e_\mu$; $A_\mu$ is self-adjoint, $\langle A_\mu f,h\rangle=\langle f,A_\mu h\rangle$; $\langle\delta_r,f\rangle=f(r)$; and consequently
--   $$
--   A_\mu f=\langle e_\mu,f\rangle\,e_\mu .
--   $$
--
--   The rank-one formula says that $A_\mu$ maps all of $M^\mu$ into the line $\mathbb{C}e_\mu$ and sees a vector $f$ only through the number $\langle e_\mu,f\rangle$. Together with unitarity it is the decisive ingredient in the proof that the Specht module generated by $e_\mu$ is irreducible.
--
--   **Formalization Note** $M^\mu$ is `EuclideanSpace ℂ (Tabloid μ)` with Mathlib's inner product, which is conjugate-linear in the first argument; $\rho_\mu$ is `tabloidRep`, $A_\mu$ is `antisym`, $\delta_r$ is `delta` and $e_\mu$ is `polytabloid`. The lemma `signC_conj` records that the complex sign is real.
